[614.318301, "o", "[user] curl -s file:///data/page.html\r\n[model] call curl({\"arguments\":\"-s file:///data/page.html\"})\r\n[tool curl] ok: {\"service\":\"releases\",\"version\":\"1.2\",\"notes\":\"several bug fixes and performance improvements\"}\r\n[model] Done. Here is what each step returned: curl: {\"service\":\"releases\",\"version\":\"1.2\",\"notes\":\"several bug fixes and performance improvements\"}\r\n"]
[616.335876, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# The page you saved in item 2 reads back. The tool doesn't check paths itself: the filesystem it is \r\n# given holds only that one folder.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[623.356041, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# For hosts, the grant is what stops it. `make prove` also runs a control with `allowedHosts` widened \r\n# to `*` (`control-wide-open.yaml`), and the same request to the attacker's server then goes through \r\n# (`control-checks.txt`). The tool didn't change; only its grant did.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[634.373083, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# The later steps use the prebuilt curl. To keep your own build, change the `curl` image in that \r\n# step's manifest to `tool-curl:mine`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[640.3887, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# Full transcript (prebuilt curl): `expected-output.txt`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[642.402086, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;36m# Step 3: Give the agent sqlite, then try its escapes\u001b[0m\r\n"]
