[805.840983, "o", "\r\n"]
[806.048309, "o", "workloaddeployment.runtime.wasmcloud.dev/toolbox-agent configured\r\n"]
[808.222073, "o", "workloaddeployment.runtime.wasmcloud.dev/toolbox-agent condition met\r\n"]
[810.248495, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# The same agent as step 3, with gh added to the agent's tools, and `github-api` added next to \r\n# `github-auth` (sign-in, which every step declares) under `hostInterfaces`:\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[817.58029, "o", "\r\n\u001b[1;36m# Try each command\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[819.598436, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# This step needs the internet: the answers come live from GitHub, so yours will differ from the \r\n# samples here. Type each prompt into the chat at http://agent.localhost:28180, or run the curl \r\n# command under it.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[828.617856, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# 1. Sign in (optional): click Sign in with GitHub at the top of the chat page. The button is there \r\n# in every step, but each step's manifest makes a new workload, which starts signed out, so sign in \r\n# here.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[838.641157, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# Your browser goes to github.com, which asks you to approve the \"KubeCon sandbox workshop\" app (sign \r\n# in to GitHub first if you aren't). Approve, and GitHub sends you back to the chat page, which now \r\n# says `Signed in to GitHub as <you>`. The app asks for no scopes, so the token can read only what \r\n# anyone can read.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[853.658938, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# Why sign in at all: without a token, GitHub allows 60 requests an hour, shared by everyone behind \r\n# the same IP address, which in a conference room is everyone on the Wi-Fi. Signed in, you get your \r\n# own 5,000. Each prompt below costs 1 to 3 requests, and every `gh` answer ends with how many are \r\n# left. If you'd rather not sign in, or the limit runs out, follow along with the samples here; the \r\n# refusals in prompts 6 to 8 send nothing to GitHub.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[875.68381, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# How it works. This is GitHub's OAuth web application flow, with PKCE. The plugin makes a random \r\n# `state` and a secret verifier, and sends your browser to GitHub with the state and a hash of the \r\n# verifier. GitHub sends your browser back to `http://agent.localhost:28180/github/callback` with the \r\n# same state and a code. The harness passes both to the plugin, which checks the state and trades the \r\n# code for a token, proving it holds the verifier and the OAuth app's client secret. So the agent \r\n# does see one thing: that code. It works once, for a few minutes, and only together with the \r\n# verifier and the secret, which never leave the plugin. The token itself never reaches the agent.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[905.700712, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# In Codespaces, or without a browser on the same machine, the browser can't come back to \r\n# `agent.localhost`, so sign in from the terminal with GitHub's device flow instead:\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[913.717521, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# Open the link in any browser, on any device, enter the code and approve. `make github-sign-out` \r\n# signs out.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[918.739752, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# The same with curl. Start sign-in, which prints the code and the link:\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[922.759981, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# Enter the code, then check; this prints `{\"login\":\"<you>\",\"state\":\"signed-in\"}`:\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[925.777536, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# Sign out:\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[926.796986, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# Use the browser when you can. GitHub lets one OAuth app start only 50 device flow sign-ins an hour, \r\n# for everyone using it, and a full room would run through that. The browser flow has no such limit.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[936.816829, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# 2. The open pull requests (allowed) \u001b[0m\r\n"]
