[1037.035721, "o", "[user] gh api -X POST /repos/wasmCloud/wasmCloud/issues/5885/comments\r\n[model] call gh({\"arguments\":\"api -X POST /repos/wasmCloud/wasmCloud/issues/5885/comments\"})\r\n[tool gh] error: {\"access-denied\":\"github-api plugin: only GET requests are allowed; POST /repos/wasmCloud/wasmCloud/issues/5885/comments refused\"}\r\n[model] Done. Here is what each step returned: gh: ERROR: {\"access-denied\":\"github-api plugin: only GET requests are allowed; POST /repos/wasmCloud/wasmCloud/issues/5885/c\r\n"]
[1039.049383, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# `gh api` sends exactly this request through the capability, past the tool's own commands (`gh pr \r\n# merge` and the other writes are refused by the tool's own code first). The plugin refuses it before \r\n# anything leaves the host: `access-denied: github-api plugin: only GET requests are allowed`. That \r\n# holds when you are signed in, too: the plugin checks the method and the path before it looks for a \r\n# token. A read outside the allowlist is refused the same way. `gh api /user`, which would say whose \r\n# token it is, gets `github-api plugin: /user is not on this plugin's path allowlist`.\u001b[0m\r\n"]
[1064.068791, "o", "\u001b[0;33m# 8. What the tool can touch (the imports)\u001b[0m\r\n"]
